\description{
This function fits a continuous-time movement model, using the package
\code{ctmm}, to each track of a tibble of tracks. The fitted models are returned
in a tibble with one row per track, in the same order as the tracks in the
metadata (see \code{\link[=show_meta]{show_meta()}}), so that they can be easily combined with the
metadata for further analyses. The table of models is the starting point
for the analyses that rely on \code{ctmm}, such as the estimation of home ranges
with autocorrelated kernel density estimation (\code{\link[=hr_tt_akde]{hr_tt_akde()}}).
}
\details{
Fitting is done in two steps, as recommended by \code{ctmm}: first,
initial values of the parameters are guessed from the empirical variogram
of each track (\code{\link[ctmm:ctmm.guess]{ctmm::ctmm.guess()}}), and then the model is fitted by
maximum likelihood (\code{\link[ctmm:ctmm.fit]{ctmm::ctmm.fit()}}). With \code{model = "auto"} (the
default), a series of candidate models is fitted and the best one is
selected based on an information criterion (\code{\link[ctmm:ctmm.select]{ctmm::ctmm.select()}}).
This is the most appropriate approach in most cases, but it can be slow
for long tracks. It is always good practice to inspect the fit of the
models (e.g. by comparing the variogram of each track with the fitted
model, see the "Movement models with ctmm" article).

Models are fitted to each track separately. To fit models at a
different level (e.g. to each trip, or to each season of a track), first
make that level the track of \code{x}, e.g. with \code{\link[=tt_split_trips]{tt_split_trips()}} or
\code{\link[move2:mt_set_track_id]{move2::mt_set_track_id()}}.

The track data are converted with \code{\link[=mt_as_telemetry]{mt_as_telemetry()}}, so \code{x} must be in a
projected coordinate reference system with units in metres (see
\code{\link[=mt_as_telemetry]{mt_as_telemetry()}} for details).

If the model cannot be fitted for a track (e.g. because the track has too
few locations), a warning is given, and the track is kept in the table
with a \code{NULL} model, and the error message in column \code{fit_message}, so
that the table always has one row per track in the metadata. Warnings
raised by \code{ctmm} while fitting are reported with the id of the track.
}
